    "title": "Penn State loses freshman guard Kayden Mingo to transfer portal - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Paul White ,\n11 hours ago Feb 28, 2026; University Park, Pennsylvania, USA; Penn State Nittany Lions guard Kayden Mingo (4) gestures while moving with the ball during the first half against the Iowa Hawkeyes at Bryce Jordan Center. Mandatory Credit: Matthew O'Haren-Imagn Images By Matthew O'Haren, Matthew O'Haren-Imagn Images The 2026-27 season will be another year of learning new names and faces for Penn State basketball fans. Two of the team’s top players, Melih Tunca and Freddie Dilione V , entered the transfer portal after a miserable 12-20 season. While the Nittany Lions have made a couple of under-the-radar additions , they’ve lost more than they’ve gained, and things are continuing to trend in that direction.\nKayden Mingo, a promising freshman guard, entered the transfer portal today , becoming the fifth Penn State player to transfer in the weeks since last season ended. Mingo showed a lot of potential, averaging 13.7 points, 3.5 rebounds, and 4.3 assists per game in his first college season.\nThe freshman guard entering the portal means that three of the Nittany Lions ’ top five scorers are in the portal. That adds up to more than 30 points per game missing from a team that already had difficulty scoring. Nobody thought this offseason would be easy for Penn State, but the challenges just keep on piling up.\nFollow Nittany Lions Wire on X and Facebook .\nThis article originally appeared on Nittany Lions Wire: Penn State loses freshman guard Kayden Mingo to transfer portal",
    "summary": "11 hours ago Feb 28, 2026; University Park, Pennsylvania, USA; Penn State Nittany Lions guard Kayden Mingo (4) gestures while moving with the ball during the first half against the Iowa Hawkeyes at Bryce Jordan Center. Two of the team’s top players, Melih Tunca and Freddie Dilione V , entered the transfer portal after a miserable 12-20 season. Mingo showed a lot of potential, averaging 13.7 points, 3.5 rebounds, and 4.3 assists per game in his first college season. Nobody thought this offseason would be easy for Penn State, but the challenges just keep on piling up.",
